WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.367
Back with our

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:00.834
world lead

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:01.935
and the sad news

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.503
in that search for the missing sub.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:04.771
The Coast Guard

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.239
now says debris was found

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.207
on the sea floor

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.676
after a, quote, catastrophic implosion

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.878
Before this news, CNN spoke

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.580
to a former subcontractor

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.115
who worked on developing the sub.

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:17.250
And the subcontractor told CNN

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.752
that some of the construction

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:20.320
materials and design choices

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.389
were considered controversial

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.791
when the vessel was being built in 2018.

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:25.925
CNN's Veronica

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:26.860
Miracle joins us now

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.560
live from Everett,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.095
Washington, where Ocean Gate

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:29.829
is headquartered.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:30.330
And Veronica,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.032
why exactly were these choices

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:34.434
controversial at the time?

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:37.170
Well, Jake, deja vu,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.371
that subcontractor,

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.572
that subcontractor

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:41.641
who was actually part of the development

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:43.143
and the testing of the titan

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.712
here in Everett, Washington, back in 2018

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.148
tells me that these materials

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:50.083
and the design choices that they use

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:51.451
were controversial

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:52.652
not because they were innovative,

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:54.354
but because they were not tried

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:56.089
and true methods.

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:57.490
He tells me they were also working

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.226
very quickly to develop this submersible.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.227
He said what they were

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.462
trying to accomplish

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.464
was to create a lightweight submersible

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:06.833
well, that could fit a lot of people.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.034
And one of the decisions

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:08.435
they made

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:11.604
was to use carbon fiber for the hole.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:12.305
And he said

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.407
this is not a conventional application

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.542
of that material.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:16.743
A conventional application

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.445
would be like on an airplane,

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.380
and the material is under tension,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.748
but in a submersible,

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:22.982
it is under pressure.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.051
The exact opposite force.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:25.885
And there's not

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:27.420
a lot of testing around this.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.822
Here's what else he had to say about it.

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.792
They created a pressure

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:36.362
hole and took it to Woods Hole Institute

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:39.032
and subjected to the pressures

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.568
that you would find at depth

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.369
where Titanic is.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.372
And it passed But then the question

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.340
is, well,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.641
if you do that repeatedly,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:49.776
then what happens?

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.178
So these are the sorts of questions

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.747
that if you have a long

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.683
research and development program,

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:57.951
you start answering.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:59.319
But if you

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:02.222
really are pushing the envelope,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:04.124
there's no time to

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.125
you know, you're

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:08.761
answering those questions in real time.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:10.797
I have learned

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:12.265
said that he understood

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.200
Stockton Russia's mission in Ocean Gates

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.702
mission was to explore

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:17.871
uncharted waters in uncharted

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:18.605
and undiscovered

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.406
areas of the ocean, in fact.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.407
Adventure tourism,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.243
he said, was not the goal.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:24.410
It was a means to an end

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.379
in order to fund the research

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.247
that they wanted to do.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:28.982
And he believed in that vision.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.150
But I did ask him

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.317
if he would have gotten

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:32.552
on that submersible,

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.786
the Titan, and gone down

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.488
to the depths of the Titanic.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.122
And he said,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.957
as someone with an engineering background

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.859
and who worked on this development

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:40.426
said he just

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.695
would not have been comfortable Jake.

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.931
Veronica Miracle in Everett, Washington,

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.065
thanks so much.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.201
Joining us now to discuss is journalist

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:48.835
David Pogue.

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:50.270
He went out with Ocean Gate

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.906
last year for a story on CBS.

00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:55.241
David, we're talking now about the Coast

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.009
Guard theory

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:58.144
that there was a catastrophic implosion.

00:02:58.144 --> 00:02:59.445
You were on the vessel.

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:01.381
Do you have any idea what

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:02.182
might have happened

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.284
to cause that catastrophic implosion?

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.086
I have a thought.

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:08.454
I know that the

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:12.091
the turning media narrative is that Rush

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:15.461
was this crazy guy going off half cocked.

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:16.596
Please remember that

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:17.997
he designed this one of a kind

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.065
carbon fiber

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:19.999
submersible

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.635
with consultation from NASA and Boeing.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.304
There were tests.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.705
There were prototypes.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.874
I walked around in his workshop with him,

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:29.042
and he showed me

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.643
some of the previous carbon

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:32.745
fiber experiments

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:35.148
that he had refined and improved

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.083
until the carbon fiber

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:37.517
was five

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:38.551
inches thick,

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.620
unbroken by any holes or wires

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.855
or anything, any screws.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:43.823
However,

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:46.993
this submersible had titanium and caps

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.463
and a nine inch thick Plexiglas or gold.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:53.132
You have three different materials,

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.435
whereas all existing

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:57.537
Titanic, Titanic submersibles

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:59.973
are just spheres made of titanium alloy

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:01.474
one material.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:02.542
And the risk here

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:04.410
is that through repeated

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.945
descent and rise, descent

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:09.048
and rise the difference in their density,

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:10.083
the difference in the amount

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:11.884
they heat up and cool down

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.020
during those things might have introduced

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.422
some little cracks, some little gap.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:17.490
And remember,

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.158
as we know, those pressures,

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.661
if a molecule

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.497
of water gets in, it's over instantly.

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:25.498
I know it's

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:26.399
no great comfort

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.334
to the families and spouses,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.903
but they did die instantaneously.

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:32.338
They were not even aware

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.906
that anything was wrong.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:35.008
So you went on the sub,

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:36.075
but once you were just

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.777
37 feet underwater,

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:40.113
your trip was was aborted.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.482
What was it like being inside the sub?

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:43.716
Was it cramped?

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:44.951
Was it uncomfortable?

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:46.085
Was it exciting?

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.787
What was it like?

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:52.292
Imagine a tubular minivan without seats.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:53.326
That's what it's like.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.561
You're you're sitting on the floor

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:56.896
there's four people

00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:57.930
alternating right, left,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.333
right, left with their legs in parallel.

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.901
And then Stockton, the pilot,

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.637
sits on his back against the back.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.638
Driving the thing.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.408
And it's very modern, looking,

00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:10.910
very cool lighting music system.

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:13.179
It's quite a departure

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:16.149
from the complicated beeping pinging

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:18.284
submersibles of old.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:20.353
And, you know, everyone says,

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:22.455
you know, stop and rush, you fool.

00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:24.757
But he said to me,

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:25.958
yes, when I designed this

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.093
with carbon fiber,

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.661
everybody said I was an idiot.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.963
They said I was nuts was his word.

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.633
And then his famous line was,

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:36.102
but when you think outside the box,

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:37.537
everybody who's inside

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:40.139
the box thinks you're crazy.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.542
So you also wrote in your piece

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:43.176
for CBC

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.610
about how the sub was lost

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.813
for a few hours last year.

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:47.447
When you were there,

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:49.515
you said the ship was able to send short

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.751
texts to the sub,

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:52.518
but it didn't know

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:54.520
where the sub was underwater.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.522
And I wonder if, looking back now,

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.558
are you sure that ocean gate?

00:05:58.558 --> 00:05:59.792
Are you sure that Stockton Rush,

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.361
we're giving you the whole story

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:06.799
I can't read his mind,

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.501
but I will say this.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.169
I know for a fact

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:13.172
that there are glitches and malfunctions

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:14.674
and weather delays

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:16.008
in every single

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.043
one of these submersibles,

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:19.312
the ones that James Cameron took down,

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:21.147
those had glitches and malfunctions

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:22.215
and were canceled.

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:24.050
The ones that would hole users.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:24.951
It is

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:27.487
these things are one offs right there.

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:28.855
They're custom built.

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:31.157
There wasn't a 1.0 and this is 2.0.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.493
There's no spare. There's one of it.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.061
So, yeah, things

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:35.995
get jerry-rigged

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:38.431
and things get fixed on the fly.

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.800
But they all do this.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.002
And I got to believe

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:45.204
that for the people who sign up for this,

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:46.172
these are the kinds of people

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:47.407
who go climb Mount

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.408
Everest or swim

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:49.509
with the sharks

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:51.811
or right on the Blue Origin rockets

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:53.913
It is the danger

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:55.681
that makes it appealing to them.

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.916
Well, speaking of signing up for it,

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:58.084
you noted in your piece

00:06:58.084 --> 00:06:59.185
that you signed a waiver

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:00.486
that mentioned death,

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:03.689
possible death as a risk multiple times.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:05.825
You sign this before getting in this job.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.760
Did you seriously consider

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.895
that something could go

00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:11.197
catastrophically wrong as has happened

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:11.731
this time?

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.102
I mean, emotionally, I was terrified.

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:18.070
I didn't sleep the night before,

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:19.338
but intellectually,

00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:21.174
all the boxes were checked

00:07:21.174 --> 00:07:22.542
You know,
I have had a tour of this thing.

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:23.443
I saw the redundant

00:07:23.443 --> 00:07:24.911
air systems, the redundant

00:07:24.911 --> 00:07:26.212
ballast systems,

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.581
redundant computer systems.

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:30.383
I saw what a safety culture

00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:31.517
they have in that outfit.

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:32.785
They do checklists

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:33.486
and briefings,

00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:35.822
inspections before and after every dove.

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:38.191
And this is the key.

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:39.392
At that time,

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:43.062
the sub had made 20 uneventful dives

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:45.698
to titanic depths and back.

00:07:46.265 --> 00:07:48.334
And that's what reassured me.

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.602
But of course,

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.871
now, in hindsight, I see that

00:07:51.871 --> 00:07:53.005
that repeated

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:55.675
rise and fall may have been its downside.

00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:57.543
David Pogue,

00:07:57.543 --> 00:07:58.110
thank you so much

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.212
for sharing your experience.

00:07:59.212 --> 00:08:00.513
And insight with us. We appreciate it.

